\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Reference geometry. (a) Axial projection of the scalar line channel, one three-dimensional translated simple-cubic vector bulk, and the finite six-segment interaction slab. The source-link motif shown on one segment is repeated on every active segment. Representative bulk axial and face-diagonal bonds are keyed by $K_{\rm  axis}$ and $K_{\rm  diag}$. (b) Transverse section at a segment midpoint. The four corners are existing bulk nodes at $(s_y a/2,s_z a/2)$; each red radius is the projection of two distinct endpoint-to-corner links. (c) Inset for one corner, showing endpoint separation $a$ and the two source-link rest lengths $\sqrt  3a/2$. Panel (a) is a projection of a single three-dimensional vector lattice, not a collection of independent planes.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sectionII_geometry}{{1}{3}{Reference geometry. (a) Axial projection of the scalar line channel, one three-dimensional translated simple-cubic vector bulk, and the finite six-segment interaction slab. The source-link motif shown on one segment is repeated on every active segment. Representative bulk axial and face-diagonal bonds are keyed by $K_{\rm axis}$ and $K_{\rm diag}$. (b) Transverse section at a segment midpoint. The four corners are existing bulk nodes at $(s_y a/2,s_z a/2)$; each red radius is the projection of two distinct endpoint-to-corner links. (c) Inset for one corner, showing endpoint separation $a$ and the two source-link rest lengths $\sqrt 3a/2$. Panel (a) is a projection of a single three-dimensional vector lattice, not a collection of independent planes}{figure.1}{}}
