\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Bulk modes and exact centered core symmetry. (a) Exact bulk branches along $\Gamma _\perp (0,0)\rightarrow X_\perp (\pi /a,0)\rightarrow M_\perp (\pi /a,\pi /a)\rightarrow \Gamma _\perp (0,0)$ at fixed $k_x=\kappa _q$. The horizontal line $\omega =1.2$ intersects both lower branches. The quoted upper minimum $1.598816$ comes from a separate full two-dimensional transverse-zone optimization, not from assuming that the plotted path is exhaustive. The upper branch is kinematically unavailable only when $k_x=\kappa _q$ is conserved; the finite slab does not conserve $k_x$. (b) Normalized axial and radial $A_1$ patterns on one four-corner cross section. Six segments give $6\times 4\times 3=72$ core coordinates but only $6\times 2=12$ centered $A_1$ coordinates. Arrow lengths are schematic symmetry patterns, not physical relative amplitudes or polarization controls.}}{8}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:s4-bulk-symmetry}{{3}{8}{Bulk modes and exact centered core symmetry. (a) Exact bulk branches along $\Gamma _\perp (0,0)\rightarrow X_\perp (\pi /a,0)\rightarrow M_\perp (\pi /a,\pi /a)\rightarrow \Gamma _\perp (0,0)$ at fixed $k_x=\kappa _q$. The horizontal line $\omega =1.2$ intersects both lower branches. The quoted upper minimum $1.598816$ comes from a separate full two-dimensional transverse-zone optimization, not from assuming that the plotted path is exhaustive. The upper branch is kinematically unavailable only when $k_x=\kappa _q$ is conserved; the finite slab does not conserve $k_x$. (b) Normalized axial and radial $A_1$ patterns on one four-corner cross section. Six segments give $6\times 4\times 3=72$ core coordinates but only $6\times 2=12$ centered $A_1$ coordinates. Arrow lengths are schematic symmetry patterns, not physical relative amplitudes or polarization controls}{figure.3}{}}
